--TEST--
A user-declared class constant reads at its own declared type through `extends`, `implements` and `self::`, and lowers to its value — `rule:types/declaration`, `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/constants-and-class
// A user-declared class constant carries its own declared type, and that type
// is what a read has — not the type its literal would have on its own. ADR
// 0007 § 1's "nothing is untyped" is what makes the annotation load-bearing:
// `Limits::WIDTH` is a `uint` because the declaration says so, and `5` placed
// anywhere else would have been an `int`.
interface Bounded {
    public const uint CEILING = 100;
}

class Limits implements Bounded {
    public const uint WIDTH = 5;
    public const string LABEL = "limits";
    public const bool STRICT = true;

    // `self::` reaches the same constant from inside the declaring class, and
    // reads it at the same declared type.
    public static function width(): uint { return self::WIDTH; }
}

// A subclass declares none of its own: every one of these reads through
// `extends`, and through `implements` for the interface's.
final class Narrow extends Limits {}

uint $own = Limits::WIDTH;
uint $inherited = Narrow::WIDTH;
uint $throughInterface = Narrow::CEILING;
uint $throughSelf = Limits::width();
string $label = Limits::LABEL;
bool $strict = Limits::STRICT;

echo $own, " ", $inherited, " ", $throughInterface, " ", $throughSelf, "\n";
echo $label, " ", $strict as string, "\n";

// The declared type is what a *use* is checked against, so the constant is
// usable everywhere a `uint` is — arithmetic included.
uint $sum = Limits::WIDTH + Narrow::CEILING;
echo $sum, "\n";
--EXPECT--
5 5 100 5
limits 1
105
